WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:09.960
Cookie Banners are possibly one of the most&nbsp;
noticeable implementations of data protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:16.160
legislation in a person's everyday life. You&nbsp;
probably see them as an annoying popup that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:22.760
hinders your ability to get to content quickly,&nbsp;
However for a company cookies serve as a useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:28.080
tool to measure engagement with its website and&nbsp;
for content publishers selling their space to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:35.400
advertisers cookies are a mean of determining how&nbsp;
much you as a visitor are worth for advertisers.&nbsp;

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:41.640
These advertisers engage in programmatic&nbsp;
real time bidding to win a split second chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:48.320
to promote a product it thinks you're likely&nbsp;
to click on. I personally never intentionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:54.600
click on any ad. If a product is displayed to&nbsp;
me that I'm remotely curious about I'll make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:59.800
sure to look it up on another device another&nbsp;
browser not connected to my account and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:06.160
my cookies are purged after each session.&nbsp;
Well, that's not perfect but it's a start. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:12.320
taking the perspective of the publishers selling&nbsp;
their space to advertisers it's in their interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:19.480
to have the most visitors accept these cookies.&nbsp;
That interest however leads to companies nudging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:27.400
website visitors to accept cookies in manners&nbsp;
that are outright non-compliant. In today's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:33.680
video we'll be looking at cookie banner designs
and explaining which ones constitute compliance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:39.920
with data protection laws and which have&nbsp;
been deemed non-compliant due to the use of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:47.200
so-called dark patterns. Whether you've been making&nbsp;
those decisions in your company or not you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:54.040
find some interesting takeaways to bring back to&nbsp;
your team. Perhaps before we start let us recap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.040 --> 00:02:01.320
briefly the governing legal principles that&nbsp;
are set in plain terms for companies to abide

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:11.640
by: The use of cookies is currently regulated in&nbsp;
the EU by the 2002 ePrivacy directive updated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:18.040
in 2009 specifically its Article 5 on&nbsp;
the confidentiality of communications. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:23.600
most recent amendment requires controllers&nbsp;
to appropriately inform the users about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:30.880
placement of cookies on their device and to give&nbsp;
them the ability to opt-in to those cookies that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:36.560
are strictly that are not strictly necessary&nbsp;
for delivering the page content onto the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:43.800
user's terminal. Additionally the General Data&nbsp;
Protection Directive which was upgraded to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:52.000
regulation in 2018 defines what constitutes&nbsp;
consent. This double obligation for providing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.880
transparency and user choice has resulted in&nbsp;
the creation of cookie banners as a means of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:02.640
delivering that information and allowing&nbsp;
to opt- in to the setting of non-essential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:09.480
cookies. In North America the approach is that&nbsp;
of an opt-out while in the EU it's that of an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:15.280
opt-in which is why you see awful banners that&nbsp;
assume you're okay with the setting of cookies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:22.280
allowing you in the best of cases to refuse them.
In the EU however these same banners must provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:29.680
users the ability to opt in which means only&nbsp;
on the user's choice can the cookies be set

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:38.600
Giving users the ability to accept cookies means&nbsp;
they must have a choice on whether non-necessary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:44.480
cookies are placed on their device. This choice&nbsp;
is ultimately provided through user consent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:50.720
to be interpreted in the way that the GDPR&nbsp;
understands consent under Article 4.11 On the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:58.520
front end consent must be freely given that means&nbsp;
the user must be able to choose to proceed without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:06.360
consenting so cookie walls are illegal. Specific:&nbsp;
that means the user cannot consent to many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:13.600
purposes under the same consent mechanism or tick&nbsp;
box so requesting consent for many purposes under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:21.920
one same button is a legal. Informed: that means the&nbsp;
user must have simple information given to them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:29.920
order to make a decision to consent so a mere link&nbsp;
to the privacy or cookie notice is likely illegal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:37.680
Unambiguous: that means that the user must give a&nbsp;
clear indication of their wish to accept cookies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:45.280
so pre-ticked boxes or the setting of cookies&nbsp;
before the user makes a choice or regardless of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:51.400
whether they ignore the banner is illegal. This&nbsp;
is not rocket science yet there's still a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:57.320
of cookie walls and pre-ticked boxes out there&nbsp;
additionally to the front end considerations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.320 --> 00:05:03.040
around cookie compliance, which could be seen as&nbsp;
the visible tip of the iceberg,data controllers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:10.520
or companies developing or implementing turnkey&nbsp;
banners must make these cookie settings available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:16.360
Despite abundant clarification around these&nbsp;
requirements one of which being the EDPB

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:23.000
on consent companies&nbsp;
still unknowingly or purposefully try to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:29.800
workarounds and get users to accept cookies. This&nbsp;
has recently led to the Privacy nonprofit NYOB

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:36.360
which placed hundreds of compliance with&nbsp;
data controllers using non-compliance cookie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:42.480
banners. Despite some response from the controllers&nbsp;
a cookie banner task force was established by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:49.240
EDPB to align on what&nbsp;
constitutes deceptive practices also known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:55.840
dark patterns and to align on enforcement&nbsp;
actions. So let's find out more about dark

00:05:55.840 --> 00:06:03.520
patterns: deceptive design patterns also known&nbsp;
as dark patterns are defined by the 

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:08.600
EDPB as interfaces meant&nbsp;
to influence users into making decisions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:15.000
that are not in their best interest but in&nbsp;
that of the controller. Initially this EDPB&nbsp;

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:20.640
definition of dark patterns applied to social&nbsp;
media platforms but it's now used to describe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:25.840
deceptive cookie banner strategies, in fact&nbsp;
several studies conducted on standard cookie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:32.600
banner templates have provided specific examples&nbsp;
of these deceptive designs. Some of the patterns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:39.920
identified include the following: Interface&nbsp;
interference: refers to the manipulation of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.920 --> 00:06:47.400
interface that would privilege certain actions&nbsp;
over another by limiting the discoverability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:55.160
of the alternative option, for example  having&nbsp;
an accept all button that is easily accessible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.160 --> 00:07:01.160
and visible on top of the banner whilst the&nbsp;
option to reject all can only be found after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:09.040
scrolling past several paragraphs of text which&nbsp;
is called a false hierarchy. Another very common&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:14.360
example of false hierarchy is also designing&nbsp;
the acceptance button in a way that it stands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:20.560
out perhaps in bright colors while the other&nbsp;
options blend to the background and are less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:27.080
noticeable at first glance in comparison. This&nbsp;
type of design brings into question whether the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.080 --> 00:07:33.720
consent was truly freely given considering that&nbsp;
the data subject were nudged to make a choice and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:41.280
may be completely unaware of the possibility of&nbsp;
opting out. Sneaking: refers to hiding the relevant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:47.920
information and only providing the ability to&nbsp;
opt out behind a less visible and unformatted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:56.880
hyperlink as such it challenges the notions of&nbsp;
freely given and informed consent. Often times&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:03.920
this looks like a banner that presents a large&nbsp;
invisible accept all button and a smaller text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:11.480
reading more options or manage settings&nbsp;
displayed in a corner or not formatted as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.480 --> 00:08:18.800
a button. Additionally cookie banners continue&nbsp;
to offer pre-ticked boxes or simply interpret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.800 --> 00:08:26.080
continued scrolling as an indication of consent.&nbsp;
These two go against the requirement of consent to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:32.960
be an unambiguous indication of the user's wishes,&nbsp;
what is needed is simply clear and affirmative

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:41.160
action. A good starting point is to ensure that&nbsp;
the cookie banner meets the consent requirements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:47.760
indicated in Article 4.11 and article 7 of&nbsp;
the GDPR. You need to ask yourself whether you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:53.800
provide users enough accurate information as to&nbsp;
the placement of cookies on their device. Do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.800 --> 00:08:58.960
allow the option to choose whether to accept&nbsp;
each category of cookies in a granular manner?&nbsp;

00:08:58.960 --> 00:09:05.840
Do users tick the option themselves? Are you&nbsp;
potentially misleading users to pick one option&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:12.400
over the other? Furthermore national supervisory&nbsp;
authorities provide cookie banner guidelines which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:18.760
are of particular interest for those of you who&nbsp;
are under the jurisdiction of those countries but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:24.760
for those who aren't still provide a good basis&nbsp;
for how to ensure that the banner on your website&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:31.440
is in compliance with EU standards. Particularly&nbsp;
useful in that respect is the German good practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:37.560
Initiative for cookie banner consent management&nbsp;
design guideline. The guideline includes examples&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:44.560
of compliant designs advises on layered banners&nbsp;
and on managing user consent over time. The link&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:49.880
to these guidelines and all sources mentioned&nbsp;
in this video can be found in the description&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.880 --> 00:09:56.440
box below. Before we wrap up this video let me&nbsp;
draw your attention on three additional topics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.440 --> 00:10:03.160
that are seldom mentioned. Firstly: Article 6
of the GDPR states that processing shall be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:11.680
lawful only if and to the extent that at least&nbsp;
one legal base applies. Two options initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:18.120
stand out: user consent or legitimate interest&nbsp;
of the publisher. In the last 18 months large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:24.560
publishers most noticeably news networks have&nbsp;
thus not been presetting cookies offering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:31.840
option to unambiguously opt into the processing&nbsp;
of targeted advertising, remarketing, tracking and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:40.040
audience measurement while setting those cookies&nbsp;
undercover of legitimate interest. This exploits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.040 --> 00:10:47.040
the potential interpretation loophole created&nbsp;
by the formulation in the law of at least one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:53.560
legal base applies. Newer implementations of&nbsp;
this workaround add a touch of compliance by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:59.840
allowing the user to dig deeper and object to&nbsp;
the legitimate interest which is a data subject&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:07.280
right provided under Article 21 of the GDPR. The&nbsp;
aggravating factor however remains that of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:14.000
overarching deceitful dark pattern of making it&nbsp;
much harder to be informed about what is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:21.080
happening and simultaneously much harder to object&nbsp;
to the processing which is a blatant violation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:28.520
the fundamental principles of EU data protection&nbsp;
which are transparency and control. Secondly:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.520 --> 00:11:36.000
relative to transparency and control almost every&nbsp;
site you visit provides a banner or some form of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:43.280
informative interface that disappears once you&nbsp;
formulate your initial choice. Site publishers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:48.840
are quick to argue that they set cookies to&nbsp;
provide you with the best possible experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:55.680
but they don't inform you of what that experience&nbsp;
is, nor provide you with the means of altering your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:01.200
settings past that first choice. So if if you're&nbsp;
going to provide settings make sure they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:09.160
accessible at any time. Best practices include&nbsp;
leaving a cookie icon hovering in one corner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:16.000
of the page. Finally remember that relying on a&nbsp;
cookie banner solution even those from reputable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:23.240
providers does not make you compliant.The same&nbsp;
can be said of consent management platforms some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:29.160
of which mislead their implementers into thinking&nbsp;
their solutions have figured out compliance for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:35.760
them. Ultimately, if you provide a website your&nbsp;
data controller obligations remain unaffected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:43.960
by the use of third party tools or so-called&nbsp;
consent and transparency frameworks. If anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:49.320
your liability is increased by the fact you'll&nbsp;
be sharing data with hundreds if not thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:57.360
of third parties with little to no paperwork to&nbsp;
show for. And don't forget to keep your RoPA up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:02.960
to date. If you wish to find out more or if you&nbsp;
have any specific question perhaps you'd like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:09.400
support in reviewing your currently implemented&nbsp;
banner settings, we at TechGDPR provide ad hoc&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:14.320
consulting on European Data Protection. Thank&nbsp;
you for watching see you in the next video

